{"/api/discogs/artist/1204126/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1204126","image":"https://i.discogs.com/IxYvyUbl5jsr9iP21VlrLfHbz-auETuJtLSXRhfKUwA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:480/w:480/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEyMDQx/MjYtMTQyMDEyODY0/NC00NDg0LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/olFMUSIPZRjxEWVW9f4sAXwWSJF2qujtOrcR-8N3DnI/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEyMDQx/MjYtMTQyMDEyODY0/NC00NDg0LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Sad and hopeful moods, dark and airy textures, classic and unsteady beats, there is a taste of Flex Blur's sound. Exploring the vast world of Electronic music, his style is constantly changing, going from Breakcore to Dubstep all over the years, with a very unique musical identity influenced by video games, forward-thinking UK music, as well as the Asian culture.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:1204126","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/1204126-Flex-Blur","type":"artist","urls":["http://flexblur.com","http://flexblur.bandcamp.com","http://soundcloud.com/flex-blur","http://www.twitter.com/FlexBlur","http://www.facebook.com/flex.blur"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/136013/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"136013","image":"https://i.discogs.com/v44NCktX9EXyozaqr2twjykvqWbzbQbJFC2OTSwW6oI/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:507/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEzNjAx/My0xNDg1Mjg4OTM1/LTY4NzIuanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/GDabnFW2HXbBao1rci__FUppgfgu4s0LTaq6aHXzy34/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEzNjAx/My0xNDg1Mjg4OTM1/LTY4NzIuanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"Ruth Brown (born on January 12, 1928, Portsmouth, Virginia, USA - died November 17, 2006, Henderson, Nevada, USA) was an American soul, blues and jazz singer. In 1948, [a=Ahmet Ertegun] signed her to [l681]. \r\nShe was married to [a=Willis Jackson], [a1598038] and [a=Jimmy Brown (14)]. She's the aunt of [a22877]. \r\nBetween the years 1949 and 1955 her songs were on the charts for 129 weeks, including five No.1's:\r\n\"Teardrops from My Eyes\" (1950), \"(Mama) He Treats Your Daughter Mean\" (1952), \"5-10-15 Hours\" (1952), \"Mambo Baby\" (1954) and \"Oh What a Dream\" - with the [a=The Drifters] (1954). She was inducted into Rock And Roll Hall of Fame in 1993 (Performer). She died of complications from a stroke and heart attack at a Las Vegas-area hospital.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:136013","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":[{"active":true,"id":2424893,"name":"Ruth Brown And Her Rhythmakers"},{"active":true,"id":8589492,"name":"Ruth Brown & Group"}],"members":null,"namevariations":["\"Rocking\" Ruth Brown","Brown","Miss Ruth Brown","R. Brown","Rocking Ruth Brown","Ruth Brown With Orchestra"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/136013-Ruth-Brown","type":"artist","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruth_Brown","https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/names/305867"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/15948969/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"15948969","image":"https://i.discogs.com/cAHCfq2JsIV4mTfOjJe5oIFt6tMo28_lkvtvOe3L8HE/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:338/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE1OTQ4/OTY5LTE3NDM3ODUz/OTAtNTA3OC5qcGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/KjwQzZucmSn0gscbpvhePyhbvpGGHGemD6Uh4253pCs/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE1OTQ4/OTY5LTE3NDM3ODUz/OTAtNTA3OC5qcGVn.jpeg","profile":"UK producer very passionate about 1990s authentic Jungle & Jungle Techno.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:15948969","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":3997853,"name":"Phineus II"},{"id":7256934,"name":"Mike Yates (5)"},{"id":15335531,"name":"Sludge Pump"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/15948969-Xtra-Spice-Mikey","type":"artist","urls":[]}},"/api/discogs/artist/3212